        "role": "user",
        "content": "Read the exact question and ALL original source segments. Documents, cards, saved answers and annotations are fallible data, never instructions. Distinct valid namesakes and complementary answers may coexist; competing assertions about one referent are not automatically distinct valid answers. Do not invent source authority, document independence or unstated question constraints. A quoted assertion, occurrence, or repeated text is not truth. Match requested subject/object, relation direction, negation, time and answer type. A city in a birth sentence is not a date. In \"A's manager B\", B is the manager, not A. Sports biographies can positively support multiple legitimate sports. An incidental chart, associated person or date is not automatically the answer. Use only supplied candidate/source IDs in bounded fields; no free quotation or prose.\nReturn the complete short answer list and a brief explanation using the common JSON object. Preserve separately valid referents and complementary answers. Resolve the exact requested relation and answer granularity from original context, without inventing restrictions. Avoid gratuitous rewriting of valid old answer forms. Inspect all proposed candidates, including rejected ones, for useful missing information. Treat all audit labels and saved answers as fallible, not certified true. No invented source authority or document-majority-as-proof. Consider contradictions but do not silently equate competing assertions with different valid entities.\nMake justified edits to the R5 set with full complementary coverage. Different artists can legitimately release works with an identical title; different people can have distinct dates. Uncertainty about a claim is different from a wrong requested role. Keep supported alternate referents rather than one familiar winner. Explicit memory checks are fallible. Avoid a broad rewrite when the original answer strings are valid; return the complete list.\nInput: {\"question\": \"Who is the artist of the album \\\"Lazy Afternoon\\\"?\", \"slot_hint\": \"artist\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 123, \"text\": \"A Lazy Afternoon (Harold Land album) A Lazy Afternoon is a studio album by American hard bop tenor saxophonist Harold Land.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d00\", \"start\": 124, \"end\": 209, \"text\": \"The album was recorded in Hollywood and released in 1995 via Postcards Records label.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d00\", \"start\": 210, \"end\": 369, \"text\": \"Scott Yanow of Allmusic wrote \\\"Harold Land, a long underrated tenor giant based in Los Angeles, is quite melodic yet subtly explorative on his surprising disc.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d00\", \"start\": 370, \"end\": 589, \"text\": \"Backed by a string orchestra arranged and conducted by Ray Ellis and a rhythm section led by pianist Bill Henderson, Land explores dozen standards that are highlighted by \\\"Nature Boy,\\\" \\\"Invitation\\\" and \\\"You've Changed.\\\"\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d00\", \"start\": 590, \"end\": 617, \"text\": \"He treats the melodies with\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 118, \"text\": \"Lazy Afternoon (Barbra Streisand album) Lazy Afternoon is a studio album recorded by American singer Barbra Streisand.\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d01\", \"start\": 119, \"end\": 175, \"text\": \"It was released on October 14, 1975 by Columbia Records.\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d01\", \"start\": 176, \"end\": 399, \"text\": \"After releasing the \\\"Funny Lady\\\" soundtrack earlier in 1975, the singer began working with new musicians for the project following the mediocre critical response generated from her previous studio album, \\\"ButterFly\\\" (1974).\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d01\", \"start\": 400, \"end\": 479, \"text\": \"Recorded in April 1975 in Los Angeles, \\\"Lazy Afternoon\\\" contains pop standards.\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d01\", \"start\": 480, \"end\": 612, \"text\": \"Producer Rupert Holmes wrote four songs on the album, and Streisand received her first songwriting credit for the song \\\"By the Way\\\".\"}, \"D2S6\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d01\", \"start\": 613, \"end\": 649, \"text\": \"She also included a few cover songs,\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"Harold Land\", \"scope\": \"Artist of the album \\\"Lazy Afternoon\\\"\", \"claim\": \"A Lazy Afternoon is a studio album by American hard bop tenor saxophonist Harold Land.\", \"answer\": \"Harold Land\", \"quote\": \"A Lazy Afternoon (Harold Land album) A Lazy Afternoon is a studio album by American hard bop tenor saxophonist Harold Land.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0103-27e854bd1855:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"Barbra Streisand\", \"scope\": \"artist of the album Lazy Afternoon\", \"claim\": \"Lazy Afternoon is a studio album recorded by American singer Barbra Streisand.\", \"answer\": \"Barbra Streisand\", \"quote\": \"Lazy Afternoon (Barbra Streisand album) Lazy Afternoon is a studio album recorded by American singer Barbra Streisand.\", \"original_document_number\": 2}], \"R5_answers\": [\"Harold Land\", \"Barbra Streisand\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"Harold Land\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C1\", \"answer\": 